-#
  One log record as a table row.

  `data-row-key` is the dedupe identity the refresh controller uses to drop a row it has
  already shown when a poll batch overlaps at the cursor boundary. It comes from
  `log_row_key`, which is also what the JSON endpoint returns, so the server-rendered row
  and a live-appended row agree.

  The cells come from `log_row_cells`, the same helper the JSON endpoint uses. That is
  deliberate: the server-rendered row and a row appended by the refresh controller must
  have identical columns, and a second column list here (or in JS) is exactly how the two
  drift apart.

= v.table_row(hover: true, data: {"row-key": log_row_key(record)}) do |r|
  - cells = log_row_cells(record)
  - classes = log_row_cell_classes(record)
  - cells.each_with_index do |cell, index|
    -#
      The cell classes come from `log_row_cell_classes`, not from this template: the refresh
      endpoint publishes the same list for each row it returns, and a second place that
      decides what a cell looks like is how a live-appended row drifts from a loaded one.

      The severity stripe in particular paints the <td> itself rather than an inner element,
      so the colour fills the whole cell with no padding. The class list carries both the
      stripe's width (from the column definition) and its colour (from the record).
    - r.with_cell(class: classes[index]) do
      = render "logs/cell", cell: cell
